%language Markdown

# A heading, its marker and its text; emphasis in it stays the heading's,
# its asterisks apart
> # Title
  ^ markup.heading.marker
    ^^^^^ markup.heading
> ### Third *level*
  ^^^ markup.heading.marker
      ^^^^^ markup.heading
            ^ punctuation.delimiter
             ^^^^^ markup.heading

# A heading underlined
> Title
  ^^^^^ markup.heading
> =====
  ^^^^^ markup.heading.marker

# A list's marker, not its text
> - one
  ^ markup.list.marker
    ^^^ -
> 2. two
  ^^ markup.list.marker

# A task's box is the list's, ticked or not; the task is text
> - [ ] todo
  ^ markup.list.marker
    ^^^ markup.list.unchecked
        ^^^^ -
> - [x] done
    ^^^ markup.list.checked
        ^^^^ -

# A quote's marker, not its text, on every line of the quote
> > quoted
  ^ markup.quote.marker
    ^^^^^^ -
> > on
  ^ markup.quote.marker

# A list item's indent on a line of its own is not a quote's marker
> - one
>   two
  ^^ -

# A quote inside a list: its marker is a quote's on every line
> - a
>   > q
    ^ markup.quote.marker
>   > r
    ^ markup.quote.marker

# A code block naming its language: the fences and the name are the
# block's, the code is the language's, plain where it names nothing
> ```rust
  ^^^ markup.raw.block
     ^^^^ markup.raw.block
> let x = 1;
  ^^^ keyword
      ^ none
          ^ number
> ```
  ^^^ markup.raw.block

# The language named by its files' extension
> ```py
> def f(): pass
  ^^^ keyword
      ^ function
> ```

# A language this build has no grammar for: the code is plain
> ```haskell
> main = pure ()
  ^^^^ none
> ```

# A block naming no language is the block's, all of it
> ```
> plain code
  ^^^^^ markup.raw.block
> ```

# A code block in a list: the item's indent is the list's, not Rust's
> - item
>   ```rust
>   let a = 1;
    ^^^ keyword
>   ```

# An indented code block
>     indented code
      ^^^^^^^^ markup.raw.block

# A block of HTML is HTML
> <div class="note">
   ^^^ tag
       ^^^^^ attribute
             ^^^^^^ string
> Text
> </div>

# The line between sections
> ---
  ^^^ punctuation.special

# Front matter at the top is YAML, its fences YAML's own
> ---
  ^^^ punctuation.special
> title: Hello
  ^^^^^ property
         ^^^^^ string
> draft: false
         ^^^^^ constant.builtin
> ---
  ^^^ punctuation.special
>
> # Heading
    ^^^^^^^ markup.heading

# Between `+++` it is TOML
> +++
> title = "Hello"
  ^^^^^ property
          ^^^^^^^ string
> draft = false
          ^^^^^ constant.builtin
> +++

# Further down the same lines are a break and text, not front matter
> Text
>
> ---
  ^^^ punctuation.special
> key: v
  ^^^^^^ -

# A link defined for reference elsewhere
> [home]: https://example.com "Home"
  ^^^^^^ markup.link.label
          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ markup.link.url
                              ^^^^^^ string

# An escaped character, where the block grammar reads it and in the text
# the inline grammar reads
> [a\]b]: /url
    ^^ escape
> a \* star
  ^ -
    ^^ escape

# Emphasis is not named, its asterisks say it
> a *b* and **c**
  ^ -
    ^ punctuation.delimiter
     ^ -
            ^^ punctuation.delimiter
              ^ -

# Code over two lines of a quote: the quote's marker on the second line
# is the block grammar's, and not the inline grammar's to read
> > `one
     ^^^ markup.raw.inline
> > two`
  ^ markup.quote.marker
    ^^^ markup.raw.inline

# Code in the text
> run `cargo test` now
      ^ punctuation.delimiter
       ^^^^^^^^^^ markup.raw.inline

# Links and images in the text
> see [the docs](https://x.org "Docs")
      ^ punctuation.delimiter
       ^^^^^^^^ markup.link.label
                 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ markup.link.url
                               ^^^^^^ string
> ![a cat](cat.png)
  ^ punctuation.delimiter
    ^^^^^ markup.link.label
           ^^^^^^^ markup.link.url
> at <https://x.org> or [home]
     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ markup.link.url
                         ^^^^ markup.link.label
> mail <me@example.com>
       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ markup.link.url
